Electrospun bamboo-like \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\) encapsulated Fe-Si-N co-doped nanofibers for efficient oxygen reduction
Zhefu Li a,b, Binling Chen c, Xianfeng Wang b, Jun Nie a, Guiping Ma a
a State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing Laboratory of Biomedical Materials, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, PR China b Key Laboratory of Textile Science & Technology, Ministry of Education, College of Textiles, Donghua University, Shanghai 201620, PR China c College of Engineering, Mathematics and Physical Sciences, University of Exeter, Exeter EX4 4QF, UK
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
The ORR mechanism of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800.
## A R T I C L E I N F O
Article history:  Received 17 December 2018  Revised 17 March 2019  Accepted 24 March 2019  Available online 25 March 2019
Keywords:  Electrospinning  Electrospun fibers  Bamboo-like structure  Oxygen reduction reaction
## A B S T R A C T
In this work, a novel catalyst of oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) was designed and fabricated by electrospinning, photo-curing and carbonization, based on polymer poly(dimethylsilylene ethynylene聚enyleneethylylene) (PMSEPE) and metal organic frameworks (MOFs)—Materiaux de l'Institute Lavoisier-101(Fe) (MIL-101(Fe)). The optimal catalyst PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800, which was carbonized at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), presented a bamboo-like nanofiber structure with a high degree of graphitization, a high specific surface area \((302.3\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) and a high yield \((64.17\%)\). \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}\) particles were found to be encapsulated into the Fe-Si-N co-doped carbon nanofibers. In the electrochemical tests, PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 showed an onset potential of \(0.935\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) and a diffusion limit current of \(6.01\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (vs. RHE) in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH aqueous solution, of which performances are competitive to commercial 20 wt% Pt/C. PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 also exhibited a higher methanol tolerance and duration stability. Further Zn-air battery test used by PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 suggested its potential for the application of fuel cell.
