## 1. Introduction
Oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is one of the important reactions in the fields of fuel cells and metal-air cells [1,2]. Platinum
(Pt) and platinum-based are chosen as the efficient electrocatalysts because of their high activities. However, their high cost limited the development of fuel cells and metal-air cells [3,4]. Therefore, noble metal based catalysts have to be reduced or replaced for a
large-scale commercialization. Up to now, more and more researches have been devoted to the exploration of transition-metal-nitrogen-carbon (M-N-C) electrocatalyst for ORR due to its high activity, durability and low cost [5–7], but the mechanism of M-N-C electrocatalyst is still controversial. In addition, it is reported that the transition metal (such as Fe [8] and Co [9]) cation is able to be doped into the carbon layer and coordinate with pyridinic nitrogen of M-N-C during the carbonization, and thus enhances the ORR activities [10].
Methods to obtain M-N-C electrocatalysts have been developed. Hu et al. synthesized the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{C}\) catalyst by a one-step pyrolysis using ferrocene and cyanamide as precursors and the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{C}\) catalyst exhibited excellent catalytic performance [11]. It was found the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\) particles played a key role in ORR, which had a synergetic interaction between the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\) and graphitic layers. However, the direct pyrolysis had disadvantages such as ununiformed structure, uncontrolled distribution of active sites and agglomeration. To overcome this, template methods were increasingly applied. Among them, the Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been attracted more attention because of their unique structures and intriguing properties [12–15]. The MOFs-derived M-N-C electrocatalysts show great potentials of ORR because of their homogeneous distribution of metal and heteroatom dopants and large surface areas [16].
To further improve the electron conductivity and prevent the collapse of MOFs during the carbonization, Ma's group used Polyacrylonitrile (PAN) and Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks (ZIFs, a special type of MOFs) as precursors to synthesize Zn-Co-ZIF-n/PAN core-shell nanofibers. The as-carbonized material exhibited excellent electrocatalyst activity and methanol tolerance [17]. The carbonized fibers could not only prevent the particle from agglomeration and collapse, but also provide channels for electronic in ORR [18]. However, normal carbon precursors such as PAN can only have a low yield of about \(40{-}50\%\) , while the poly (dimethylsilylene ethynylelphenyleneethynylene) (PMSEPE) electrospun fibers produced by our group could achieve a yield as high as \(80\%\) after carbonization [19]. Recently, the PMSEPE-based materials have shown potentials for electrocatalysis [20]. It worth noting that the ORR performance could be improved as the molecular chain of PMSEPE contains silicon atoms [21,22]. The interacted mechanism of doped silicon has been explored by hybrid DFT calculations and the result showed silicon atoms could partially change the chemisorption mode of \(0_{2}\) [23].
In this study, the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe) nanofibers were synthesized by electrospinning followed by photo-curing, where polylactide was used to improve the electrospinning ability. The as-cured nanofibers were then carbonized under various temperatures, using melamine as a nitrogen source. The effect of carbonization temperature on catalytic performance was investigated, and \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was found to be optimal. \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\) particles encapsulated into the graphitic layers with a homogeneous distribution were observed in the optimal sample PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800. Its unique structure and composition were expected to enhance the ORR performance.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Melamine, \(p\) -phthalic acid (PTA) and pentaerythritol tetra(3- mercaptopropionate) were purchased from Aldrich Company. N, \(N\) -dimethyl formamide (DMF), ethanol, methanol and dichloromethane were obtained from Beijing Chemical Company. Polylactide (PLA, \(M_{w}=100,000\;\mathrm{g/mol})\) , ferric chloride hexahydrate and phenylbis (2,4,6-trimethylbenzoyl)phosphine oxide were supplied
by Aladdin Company. The poly(dimethylsilylene ethynylenephenylene-ethynylene) (PMSEPE) was synthesized by our previous study [19]. All reagents were used as received.
## 2.2. Synthesis of MIL-101(Fe)
In this experiment, the MIL-101(Fe) was synthesized according to the previously reported literature [24]. The preparation process was as follows: \(2.0\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) and \(1.2\,\mathrm{g}\) of PTA were dissolved in \(90.0\,\mathrm{mL}\) DMF under ultrasonication for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) . After it was completely dissolved, the solution was added to a \(100.0\,\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-line stainless-steel autoclaves ( \(90\%\) loading) and heated at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) . The product was collected by centrifugation and washed by DMF and ethanol for 3 times. Then the product was dried under vacuum for \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) . The characterizations of MIL-101(Fe) were shown in support information.
## 2.3. Preparation of photo-cured PMSEPE/PLA/MIL-101(Fe) electrospun fibers
\(0.2\:\mathrm{g}\) MIL-101, \(0.2\,\mathrm{g}\) melamine, \(0.5\,\mathrm{g}\) PMSEPE and \(0.1\;\mathrm{g}\) PLA were dissolved in \(6.0\,g\) dichloromethane. Melamine was selected as the N source because of its suitable decomposition temperature, high N content \((66.7\%\) by mass), and low cost. After the PMSEPE and PLA were completely dissolved, \(1.0\,g\) of DMF solution was added and the spinning solution was stirred for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) , the spinning solution was then transferred to the \(5\,\mathrm{mL}\) syringe. For the process of electrospinning, the speed of the pump was \(5\,\mathrm{mL/min}\) , the speed of collect roller was \(1000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) , the collect distance was \(8\,\mathrm{cm}\) and the spinning voltage was \(15\,\mathrm{kV}.\) . After the spinning was completed, a pink fiber membrane was obtained and dried under vacuum for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(30\,^{\circ}\!C\) . The photo-cured process of PMSEPE/PLA/MIL-101 (Fe) electrospun fibers were carried out according to our previous work [19].
## 2.4. Carbonization of photo-cured electrospun fibers
The PMSEPE/PLA/MIL-101(Fe) electrospun fiber was carbonized in a quartz tube furnace (GSL-1400X, Hefei Kejing Technology Co., Ltd. China). The carbonization process consists of two steps, firstly the fiber mat was thermal cured in air at \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{2\,\mathrm{h}}\) and then carbonized in \(\Nu_{2}\) atmosphere at target temperature (600, 700, 800, 900, and \(1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) . Below \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , the temperature rate was \(2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) and the temperature rate was \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) above \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . The obtained sample was labeled as PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-600, PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-700, PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800, PMSEPE/ MIL-101(Fe)-900, PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-1000, respectively. The pure PMSEPE fiber mat without MIL-101(Fe) and the mixed powder of MIL-101(Fe) and melamine were also carbonized in the same condition as reference samples.
## 2.5. Structure characterizations
The element mappings of the samples were characterized by scanning electron microscope (SEM, S-3200N, Hitachi, Japan). Xray diffraction patterns were collected on a powder X-ray diffractometer (XRD, D8 Advance, Bruker, Germany) system in the 2θ range of \(5{-}90^{\circ}\) with Cu Kα radiation. Microscopic features of the above prepared samples were characterized by a high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM, JEM 3010, JEOL, Japan). The thermal decomposition behavior of the photo-cured fibers was determined using a thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA, Q500, TA Instruments, USA) in \(\Nu_{2}\) atmosphere over a temperature range of \(25{-}800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a heating rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) . The surface chemical composition of the surface species was analyzed by X-ray photoelectric spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250, Thermo VG Scientific, USA).
Nitrogen absorption/desorption isotherms were obtained on an analyzer (3H-2000PS1, Beishide Instrument Company) at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\). The graphitization degree was tested by Renishaw spectrometer (Invia Reflex, Renishaw, British).
## 2.6. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical measurements were characterized by the electrochemical workstation (CHI 760E, Shanghai Chenhua Instrument Co, China) and the rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE-3A, ALS, Japan) system at room temperature in \(0.10\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution with a conventional three-electrode system. The saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and Pt wire were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. The rotating ring-disk electrode \((A=0.1256\,\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) was chosen as the substrate for the working electrode. \(5.0\,\mathrm{mg}\) PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe) catalyst was dissolved in \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol and \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(5\%\) Nafion (Dupont). Each experiment used \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) catalyst solution at one time. The electrochemical measurements were carried out at room temperature after purging \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) or \(0_{2}\) for at least \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves were obtained in \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\)- and \(0_{2}\)-saturated \(0.10\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution at a scan rate of \(50\,\mathrm{mV/s}\) until to obtain a stable CV profile. Linear sweep voltammetric (LSV) curves were measured in \(0_{2}\)-saturated \(0.10\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution with a sweep rate of \(5\,\mathrm{mV/s}\) at different speed rates (400, 625, 900, 1225, 1600, 2025, and \(2500\,\mathrm{rpm}\)). The oxygen reduction current was corrected by the background current which was tested in \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.10\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution with a sweep rate of \(5\,\mathrm{mV/s}\).
The electron transfer number during ORR process was calculated according to the slopes of Koutecky–Levich (K-L) plots by the following equation [25]:
\[
\begin{array}{l}{1/j=1/j_k+1/j_d}\\{}\\{j_d=0.62nFD_{O_2}^{2/3}C_{O_2}\nu^{-1/6}\omega^{1/2}}\\{}\\{j_k=nFKC_{O_2}}\end{array}
\]
where \(j\) was the current density, \(j_{k}\) and \(j_{d}\) were the kinetic current density and diffusion-limiting current density, respectively. \(\omega\) was the angular velocity of the disk, \(n\) was the number of electrons transferred during the ORR, \(F\) was the Faraday constant (96,485 \(C/\mathrm{mol}\)), \(C_{O_{2}}\) was the bulk concentration of \(0_{2}\) \((1.2\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\), \(D_{O_{2}}\) was the diffusion coefficient of \(0_{2}\) in the electrolyte \((1.9\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{s})\), \(\nu\) was the kinetic viscosity of the electrolyte \((0.01\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{s})\), and \(K\) was the electron transfer rate constant.
The Zn-air battery using PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe) catalyst was build according to the literature [26].
## 3. Results and discussion
To confirm the structure and chemical composition of the synthesized MIL-101(Fe), SEM, FTIR and XRD analysis were carried out. As it can be seen from the Fig. S1(A) and (B), the prepared MIL-101(Fe) particles had regular octahedrons and the particle size was about \(900\,\mathrm{nm}\). Fig. S1(C) shows that some obvious absorption peaks were appeared at 3420, 1583, 1396 and \(750\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in FTIR. The broad peak of \(3420\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is associated with the stretching vibration of O-H due to the surface adsorbed water of MIL-101 (Fe). The sharp absorption peak at 1583 and \(1396\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is the asymmetric (vs C-O) and symmetrical (vs C-O) vibrations of the carboxyl group, respectively. The strong absorption peak at \(750\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be attributed to the C-H bending vibrations of the benzene [27]. Fig. S1(D) shows the XRD patterns of MIL-101(Fe), which is similar to the literature [14].
The ORR activities of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe) carbonized at different temperatures were evaluated by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in \(0_{2}\) saturated alkaline condition. As shown in Fig. S2, the best ORR catalytic performance of the sample was carbonized at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), where the onset potential and diffusion limit current is \(0.935\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) and \(6.01\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (vs. RHE), respectively. The ORR performance was improved from \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and then was dropped with the temperature increase from \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The sample PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-600 has an onset potential of \(0.673\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) and a diffusion limit current of \(2.27\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (vs. RHE); while PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-1000 has its onset potential and diffusion limit current of only \(0.682\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) and \(3.03\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (vs. RHE), respectively.
To explore the detailed chemical composition and bonding configuration of samples, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) investigations were carried out. During the carbonization, nitrogen atoms in melamine were migrated into the carbon layers of the electrospun fiber and doped in several types such as pyridinic-N \((398.7\,\mathrm{eV})\), pyrrolic-N \((400.2\,\mathrm{eV})\), and graphitic-N \((401.3\,\mathrm{eV})\) [28]. The pyridinic-N is located at the edge of the carbon layer, and the pyrrolic-N is located in the five-sided ring, which are the defect sites of the carbon layer [29]. These two types of nitrogen atoms could increase the oxygen adsorption capacity of the carbon layers and weaken the O–O bond, thus improving the ORR catalytic ability [30]. Fig. 1A–E show the N 1s high-resolution XPS spectra of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe) carbonized at various temperatures. It can be found that the content of graphitic N increased while the contents of pyridinic-N and pyrrolic-N decreased with the increase of carbonization temperature (Fig. 1F), this is because pyridinic-N and pyrrolic-N were converted to the graphitic-N atom inside the graphitic carbon layer when temperature increased [31]. Moreover, it (Fig. 1F) seems that pyrrolic-N was easier to be converted to graphitic-N compared with pyridinic-N, as pyrrolic-N content decreased faster at higher temperature.
As shown in Raman spectra (Fig. S3), the value of \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) decreased with the increase of temperature from 600 to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), indicating that the degree of graphitization was increased. It's worth noting that, at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-900 has a higher \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) value due to the heteroatom-dopant such as S-dopant from PETMP. The XPS results (Fig. S4) show that the chemical states of S changes from 800 to \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). It suggests the decrease in the degree of graphitic carbon at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In addition, the amount of graphitic-N (nitrogen bonded to three \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) carbon atoms) is independent of S-dopant. The amount of graphitic N atoms incorporated into the carbon network of graphene increases with the increase of temperature.
Based on the XPS and Raman spectral results above, the ORR performance of samples can be explained as: the low catalytic abilities of the samples carbonized at high temperatures (PMSEPE/MIL-101 (Fe)-900 and PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-1000) could be due to the low contents of pyridinic-N and pyrrolic-N, while the unsatisfactory catalytic performance of the samples carbonized below \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-600 and PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-700) could be attributed to the lower degree of graphitization.
[32,34]. As shown in Fig. 2C, the Si 2p spectrum can be deconvoluted to C-Si-O \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) (102.16 eV), C-Si-O \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) (103.80 eV) and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\,2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) (106.88 eV) [21], suggesting the presence of Si–C bond. The Si–C bond can partially change the chemisorption mode of \(O_{2}\) and facilitate the ORR process [23]. Fig. 2D shows the XRD patterns of the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800. The typical crystallization peaks of the graphitized carbon (PDF 99-0057) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}\) (PDF 76-1877) can be clearly identified.
The HRTEM image shown in Fig. 3A, presents a bamboo-like fiber structure of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800. Such structure could improve the specific surface area. The diameter of the fibers ranges from 300 to \(500\,\mathrm{nm}\) . Fig. 3B shows that the surface of the particle was coated with layers of carbon. The lattice distance of particle is \(0.207\,\mathrm{nm}\) , corresponding to the (022) crystal plane of the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}\) phase (Fig. 3C) and the lattice width of the graphitized carbon layer is \(0.348\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 3D), which are in consistent with the XRD results.
Such bamboo-like fiber structure can offer a synergetic interaction between the carbide and graphitic layers, further enhance the ORR activity [11].
The EDS mappings of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 (Fig. 4) show that the elements C (red), Fe (green) and N (yellow) were distributed through the whole fiber. It seems that Fe element moved
from the MIL-101(Fe) crystals to the whole fiber during the carbonization. The Fe and N distributions indicate the formation of the Fe-N-C, which is in agreement with the HRTEM and XPS.
Fig. 5A shows the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isothermal adsorption-desorption curve of the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800. It exhibits a typical type-IV adsorption isotherm combined with a type-H4 hysteresis loop. The rapid increase of the curve at around \(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0\) suggests the presence of a large number of micropores; and the type-H4 hysteresis loops imply the structure of pores were slit-shaped (inset of Fig. 5A). The surface area of the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 is \(302.3\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), and the pore volume is \(119.4\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\). This porous structure can not only increase the number of active sites but also provide efficient transport channels for oxygen, thus improving the catalytic ORR ability. The TGA and DTG (shown in Fig. 5B) investigate the formation of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 catalysts during carboniza
tion. The DTG curve shows that the thermal weight loss of the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 can be divided into three stages: (1) the weight loss at \(100{-}300\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is from the release of solvents and the unreacted photo-crosslinker PBTMP; (2) the weight loss between 300 and \(500\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is from the residual PLA, the photocrosslinked network structure and the MIL-101(Fe) [35]; (3) the weight loss above \(500\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is from the carbon and hydrogen elements on the side chain of PMSEPE. In addition, it's worth noting that the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 has a high carbonization yield of \(64.17\%\).
Fig. 6 shows the electrochemical evaluations of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 and commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst. At a rotation rate of \(1600\;\mathrm{rpm}\) (Fig. 6A), the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 exhibited an onset potential of \(0.935\;\mathrm{V}\), which is similar to that of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst. Moreover, the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 is able to
achieve a remarkably high diffusion limit current of \(6.01\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) , which is higher than that of the commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst. An obvious peak can be observed in the CV curve (the inset of Fig. 6A) for the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 in the \(0_{2}\) saturated alkaline solution, indicating the pronounced ORR activity. To investigate the ORR kinetics of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800, LSV measurements were performed under different rotating speeds ranging from 400 to 2500 rpm (the inset of Fig. 6B). The current density was increased with the increase of rotating speed because of the shortened diffusion distance. The ORR reaction mechanism was further explored by the Koutecky-Levich (K-L) equation, which is used to calculate
the electron-transfer number of ORR reaction. The near parallelism properties and good linearity of \(\mathrm{K}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{L}\) plots (shown in Fig. 6B) indicate the first-order reaction kinetics. The calculated electron transfer number is 3.79, suggesting that the ORR reaction process is close to the ideal \(4\mathrm{e^{-}}\) ORR process.
The electrochemical results show that the PMSEPE/MIL-101 (Fe)-800 catalyst has the best ORR performance than other reference samples (Fig. S5), where it exhibited a superior onset potential and diffusion limit current. The reference samples, carbonized from the pure PMSEPE fiber mat without Fe element and the mixed powder of MIL-101(Fe) and melamine without Si
element, showed lower catalytic performances than the PMSEPE/ MIL-101(Fe)-800. It confirms the advantages of the presence of Si, Fe and N for ORR.
The durability and methanol crossover of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)- 800 and commercial Pt-C catalyst were tested at \(-0.4\,\mathrm{V}\). The PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 exhibited more stable performance (83% of its initial activity) than that of Pt-C catalyst (79% of its initial activity) after 18,000 s (Fig. 6C). The methanol crossover tests (Fig. 6D) show that the PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 had a negligible change in the relative current after the addition of 1 mL methanol into the electrolyte, however, Pt-C catalyst had a sharp decrease of relative current after the addition of 1 mL methanol. Thus, the above results suggest the superior durability and methanol crossover stability of PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800.
The Zn-air battery was built by using PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 and the result is shown in Fig. S6. The voltage of single cell was measured as \(1.44\,\mathrm{V}\) and two batteries in series could light up a LED bulb. Such performance suggests the potential applications in the fuel cell field.
The superior catalytic performance of the PMSEPE/MIL-101 (Fe)-800 can be attributed to the following reasons (Fig. 7): (I) The high content of pyridinic-N and pyrrolic-N changed the electron cloud distribution in the carbon layer and weakened the oxygen-oxygen bond; (II) The highly graphitized carbon layer had a good conductivity for electrons; (III) The synergistic effect between the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\) and the Fe-N-C structure in carbon layer improved the activity; (IV) The interacted effect of Si atom changed the chemisorption mode of Si; (V) The porous structure provided oxygen-conducting channels and active reaction site positions of ORR; (VI) The fibrous structure provided a channel for electronic conduction and prevented the particle agglomeration.
## 4. Conclusions
On the basis of the previous preparation methods [1–10], this work has reported that bamboo-like \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\) encapsulated Fe-Si-N co-doped nanofiber catalysts with a high yield (64.17%) could be prepared by electrospinning, photo-curing and carbonization. The optimal catalyst PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 exhibited an excellent electrocatalytic ORR performance, where the onset potential is \(0.935\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) and a diffusion limit current is \(6.01\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (vs. RHE) in 0.1 M KOH. PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 also had a better methanol tolerance and duration stability than those of commercial Pt/C. Compared with other reported ORR catalysts [9,21– 23,28–30], the advantages of our PMSEPE/MIL-101(Fe)-800 could be due to its unique structure and composition: (1) The high content of pyridinic-N and pyrrolic-N; (2) The high graphitized degree and high specific surface area; (3) The formation of Fe-N-C in carbon layer; (4) The Si-dopant; (5) The fibrous and porous fiber structure.
